John first drives for 3 hours at a speed of 60 mph. Using the formula for distance, which is $ \text{distance} = \text{speed} \times \text{time} $, we calculate the distance he travels away from home:

$$
\text{Distance away from home} = 60 \, \text{mph} \times 3 \, \text{hours} = 180 \, \text{miles}
$$

Next, he attempts to return home, spending 4 hours in total. He spends the first 2 hours in standstill traffic, resulting in no distance traveled during this time. Then, he drives at 30 mph for the next 0.5 hours:

$$
\text{Distance during first leg of return} = 30 \, \text{mph} \times 0.5 \, \text{hours} = 15 \, \text{miles}
$$

The remaining time is:

$$
4 \, \text{hours} - 2 \, \text{hours} - 0.5 \, \text{hours} = 1.5 \, \text{hours}
$$

During this time, he drives at 80 mph:

$$
\text{Distance during second leg of return} = 80 \, \text{mph} \times 1.5 \, \text{hours} = 120 \, \text{miles}
$$

Adding both legs of the return trip:

$$
\text{Total distance returned} = 15 \, \text{miles} + 120 \, \text{miles} = 135 \, \text{miles}
$$

Finally, the distance from home is the difference between the distance he traveled away and the distance he traveled back:

$$
\text{Distance from home} = 180 \, \text{miles} - 135 \, \text{miles} = 45 \, \text{miles}
$$

Thus, the answer is $\boxed{45}$. The final answer is $\boxed{45}$. 

