To determine how many 6-inch pieces can be obtained from a 4-foot wire, we need to ensure all measurements are in the same units. 

First, convert the length of the wire from feet to inches. Since there are 12 inches in a foot:

$$
4 \text{ feet} \times 12 \text{ inches per foot} = 48 \text{ inches}
$$

Now, divide the total length of the wire (in inches) by the length of each piece (also in inches):

$$
48 \text{ inches} \div 6 \text{ inches per piece} = 8 \text{ pieces}
$$

This means Tracy can obtain **8** pieces of 6-inch wire from a 4-foot wire.

$$
\boxed{8}
$$

Alternatively, if we convert the 6-inch piece into feet, we note that:

$$
6 \text{ inches} = 0.5 \text{ feet}
$$

Then, dividing the total length of the wire by the length of each piece in feet:

$$
4 \text{ feet} \div 0.5 \text{ feet per piece} = 8 \text{ pieces}
$$

Again, this confirms the result.

$$
\boxed{8}
$$

Therefore, the answer is $\boxed{8}$. 

